/* book Worked example: Q: A speech band of 0.3 kHz to 3.4 kHz modulates a 100 kHz carrier. Give the occupied frequencies and bandwidths of DSB-SC, the USB and LSB signals, and VSB with a 0.5 kHz vestige below the carrier. Then compare the radiated powers for a test tone if AM at m = 1 would have a 10 kW carrier.
   1. DSB-SC: LSB 100 − 3.4 = 96.6 kHz to 100 − 0.3 = 99.7 kHz, USB 100.3 kHz to 103.4 kHz; B = 2W = 6.8 kHz.
   2. USB signal: 100.3 kHz to 103.4 kHz. LSB signal: 96.6 kHz to 99.7 kHz. B = W = 3.4 kHz (3.1 kHz of it carries energy).
   3. The gap between the sidebands is 99.7 to 100.3 kHz, 0.6 kHz wide: the filter's whole transition band.
   4. VSB: the channel runs from 100 − 0.5 = 99.5 kHz to 103.4 kHz, B = W + fv = 3.4 + 0.5 = 3.9 kHz.
   5. Power: AM Pt = 10 × (1 + ½) = 15 kW; DSB-SC 10 × ½ = 5 kW; SSB 10 × ¼ = 2.5 kW.
   6. Savings against AM: DSB-SC (15 − 5)/15 = 66.7 %; SSB (15 − 2.5)/15 = 83.3 %.
   Answer: DSB-SC 96.6 to 103.4 kHz (6.8 kHz); USB 100.3 to 103.4 kHz and LSB 96.6 to 99.7 kHz (3.4 kHz); VSB 99.5 to 103.4 kHz (3.9 kHz). Powers 15, 5 and 2.5 kW: SSB saves 83.3 %. */
