/* book: lead: A modulator must make new frequencies, fc + fm and fc − fm, that are in neither input. A linear circuit cannot do this; a nonlinear one can. A diode or transistor run on the curved part of its characteristic has a current with a term in the square of its input voltage, and that square contains the product of carrier and message. A square-law modulator uses one such device and a band-pass filter to */
