/* book Worked example: Q: A device has i = 10v + 2v² mA (v in volts). Its input is v = 2 cos(2π × 100 × 10³t) + 0.5 cos(2π × 10³t) V. Find every output line, the carrier and sideband currents after a band-pass filter at 100 kHz, and m.
   1. a1 = 10 mA/V, a2 = 2 mA/V², Ac = 2 V at 100 kHz, Am = 0.5 V at 1 kHz.
   2. Linear term: 10 × 2 = 20 mA at 100 kHz and 10 × 0.5 = 5 mA at 1 kHz.
   3. DC: 2 × (2² + 0.5²)/2 = 2 × 4.25/2 = 4.25 mA.
   4. Harmonics: 2 × 2²/2 = 4 mA at 200 kHz and 2 × 0.5²/2 = 0.25 mA at 2 kHz.
   5. Sidebands: a2AcAm = 2 × 2 × 0.5 = 2 mA each, at 99 kHz and 101 kHz.
   6. After the filter (99 to 101 kHz): carrier 20 mA, sidebands 2 mA each.
   7. m = 2 × 2/20 = 0.2; check 2a2Am/a1 = 2 × 2 × 0.5/10 = 0.2. Through a 1 kΩ tank this is 20 V of carrier with 2 V sidebands.
   8. fc > 3W: 100 kHz > 3 kHz, so the 2 kHz line is far below the 99 kHz edge.
   Answer: Lines: 4.25 mA DC, 5 mA at 1 kHz, 0.25 mA at 2 kHz, 2 mA at 99 and 101 kHz, 20 mA at 100 kHz, 4 mA at 200 kHz. Output: 20 mA carrier, 2 mA sidebands, m = 0.2. */
